// F92, from the most expensive run of F91's variance wave: `a.iter()` was answered with
// "e.g. iter(a, ...)" when there was no `iter`. That run spent 194 compiler invocations and
// concluded `Iter<T>` was "unreachable from source". The hint now names the absence when the name
// is not a prelude function, and still names the call form when it is -- both halves below,
// because the quiet one is what keeps this from becoming the hedge this file removed once already.
//
// F170 renamed `iter_of_array` to `iter`, which **inverted this fixture's original example**: the
// exact call F92 reported, `a.iter()`, is now answered with `iter(a, ...)` and that advice is
// right. So `a.collect()` carries the absent half instead -- still an iterator-vocabulary name a
// cold model reaches for, still not a function -- and `a.iter()` stays to pin the other half,
// which is now the better record of F92 than the original was.
fn main() {
    val a = [1, 2]
    val b = a.collect()
    val c = a.iter()
    print(len(a))
}
